    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>After the 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> at the 17th parallel, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> ruled the South, and in October 1955 a referendum widely marred by fraud deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> and made Ngô Đình Diệm president of the new republic.</li><li>Diệm's authoritarian rule under the doctrine of Personalism ended in the Buddhist crisis and his killing in a US-backed coup in 1963; after four years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military juntas</a>, the 1967 constitution opened the Second Republic under Nguyễn Văn Thiệu.</li><li>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a> of 1973 South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> fielded about one and one-half million troops, but after US forces withdrew and aid was cut, the North's conventional invasion of 1975 ended with Dương Văn Minh's unconditional surrender on 30 April.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the referendum of 1955 that made Diệm president, over 380,000 more votes were cast than there were registered voters, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> he was credited with 133% of the vote; his American advisers had recommended a more modest margin of 60 to 70 percent.</p></aside>
    </section>

