    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>, created on 14 June 1949 through co-operation between anti-communist Vietnamese and the French during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, with the former emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> as chief of state. After the 1954 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference divided Vietnam temporarily at the 17th parallel, the State of Vietnam administered the territory to the south, which took in the southern and part of the central regions of the country. About one million people moved South during the period of relocation, among them at least 500,000 Catholics and about 200,000 Buddhists, while about 90,000 Việt Minh were evacuated to the North.</p><p>Ngô Đình Diệm, prime minister since 1954, held a referendum on 23 October 1955 asking voters to approve a republic and so remove Bảo Đại. The poll, supervised by his younger brother Ngô Đình Nhu, credited Diệm with 98 percent of the votes. On 26 October 1955 Diệm declared himself president of the newly proclaimed Republic of Vietnam, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>. He had announced in July 1955 that South Vietnam would not take part in the elections specified in the Geneva Accords, and in December 1955 he ended the economic and financial agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; the French, who needed troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, had completely withdrawn by April 1956.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> kept a capitalist free-market economy with ties to the West, greatly assisted by American aid and by the presence of large numbers of Americans between 1961 and 1973. Between 1954 and 1973 electrical production increased fourteen-fold, industrial output rose by an average of 6.9 percent a year, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> output by 203 percent, and the number of university students from 2,000 to 90,000. Diệm's land reform of October 1956 limited rice farms to 247 acres per landowner but had stalled by 1960, since many of his biggest supporters were large landowners; the Land-to-the-Tiller program of 1970 limited holdings to 15 hectares.</p><p>In 1968 the population was estimated at 16,259,334, though about one-fifth of the people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>–Dong Nai delta lived in areas controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>. In 1970 about 90% were Kinh and 10% Hoa, Montagnard, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a>, Eurasians and others. Between 3 and 4 million people were Buddhists, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caodaism">Cao Đài</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoa-hao">Hòa Hảo</a> religions had between 2.5 and 3 million followers, and about 10% of the population was Catholic. From 1955 the Diệm government pursued an assimilation policy towards the Montagnard and the Cham, banning the Cham language in public schools and seizing indigenous lands for Kinh settlers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> Military Forces were formally established on 30 December 1955 from the French-led Vietnamese National Army, itself gathered in July 1951 from colonial auxiliary units, and comprised the Army of the Republic of Vietnam (ARVN), the Air Force, the Navy and the Marine Division. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a> began in 1957, though its enforcement was lax. The French ceased training the forces in 1956, and American advisers restructured them along US lines; the country was divided into four corps tactical zones and the Capital Military District around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>.</p><p>The insurgency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>, armed and supported by North Vietnam, had by 1961 brought half the rural region south and southwest of Saigon under considerable communist control, by a US intelligence estimate. American combat troops arrived in 1965, and during the Tet Offensive of February 1968 over a million South Vietnamese soldiers served beside 500,000 US soldiers and 100,000 from allies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> in 1971 was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">PAVN</a>, and the North's conventional invasion of 1972 was repulsed only with massive US air support. In 1973 the state fielded the fourth largest military force in the world, but it depended on the US for spare parts, fuel and ammunition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was a unitary and centralised state whose politics moved from authoritarianism under the ideology of personalism (1955–1963) to fractious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military juntas</a> (1963–1967) and then to the Second Republic (1967–1975). Diệm adopted Personalism, a doctrine presented as a third way between Marxist collectivism and Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, gave key posts to his family, with Ngô Đình Nhu gaining authority over the military, police and intelligence, and built a personality cult through the Ministry of Information and Youth and the National Revolutionary Movement. US representatives reported that his government regularly ordered the execution of political opponents and shut down dissident newspapers, and peasants were forcibly relocated into "strategic hamlets".</p><p>After a series of coups, Air Marshal Nguyễn Cao Kỳ became prime minister in 1965 with General Nguyễn Văn Thiệu as figurehead chief of state. The constitution of 1967 granted freedom of speech, press and association and created a House of Representatives and a Senate. Thiệu won the presidency in elections widely considered fraudulent and was re-elected unopposed in 1971 with 94% of the vote, his rule growing more dictatorial. Amnesty International in 1973 put the number of civilian prisoners at 35,257 to 200,000 or more. The country was divided into forty-four provinces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> belonged to the Western Bloc and was a close ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which saw it as a counterbalance to communist North Vietnam. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was recognised by 95 countries as of January 1975, but its admission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> was vetoed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; it held observer status at the General Assembly and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, the World Bank, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and other agencies. Its defence drew the United States into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> on a large scale from 1965, and the Tet Offensive of 1968 led the Nixon administration to the policy of Vietnamization.</p><p>Within the country Buddhists, Catholics, students, labour unions and a non-government press formed a fragmented but active civil society. The historian Peter Zinoman sets a productive intellectual and cultural life and a republican political culture beside the regime's authoritarianism, militarism and dependency on the US, and the 'Vietnamese turn' scholarship of recent decades has challenged earlier portrayals that dwelt on its negative side. The assimilation policy in the Central Highlands fed movements such as FULRO, which fought both South Vietnam and the succeeding Socialist Republic of Vietnam until the late 1980s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a> of 27 January 1973, US forces left at the end of March 1973 while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">PAVN</a> forces in the South remained in place. The Case–Church Amendment of 4 June 1973 prohibited renewed American intervention, the oil price shock of October 1973 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> that left the government struggling to pay its soldiers, and in August 1974 Congress cut aid from $1 billion a year to $700 million. Thiệu declared on 4 January 1974 that the war had restarted, and the same month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paracel-islands">Paracel Islands</a>. Phuoc Long, abandoned in early January 1975, was the first provincial capital to fall.</p><p>The offensive at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buon-ma-thuot">Ban Me Thuot</a> opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> Campaign. Thiệu's withdrawal from the Central Highlands became a rout, Huế and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-nang">Da Nang</a> were captured, and by early April the PAVN had overrun almost three fifths of the South. After a last-ditch defence by the ARVN 18th Division at the Battle of Xuân Lộc, Thiệu resigned on 21 April and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>; Trần Văn Hương held office for a week before the assembly handed the presidency to Dương Văn Minh, who surrendered unconditionally on 30 April 1975. The Provisional Revolutionary Government then became the government of the South, which merged with the North on 2 July 1976, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> was renamed Ho Chi Minh City.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was the anti-communist state of southern Vietnam from 1955 to 1975. Born of the partition of 1954 and dependent on American aid and arms, it passed from Diệm's personalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military juntas</a> to the Second Republic, and fell to the North's invasion in April 1975.</em></p>

